#### 4.5.2 Tof \(1/\beta\)

The mass of electrons is negligible in the momentum range considered in this analysis, and as a result they have a velocity \(\beta\equiv v/c\sim 1\). Heavier particles will travel slower at a given momentum, and the TOF can be used to separate these from the electrons by measuring the particle flight time and velocity. As the momentum of particles

Figure 4.19: The height (left), mean (middle) and width (right) of the Gaussian functions fitted to the electrons (red circles), pions (blue triangles), and protons (green squares) in Au+Au collisions versus momentum.

increases, their velocity approaches \(c\) and hadrons can no longer be separated from electrons. The TOF can be used to separate electrons from heavier hadrons up to \(p\sim 1.5\) GeV/\(c\), but cannot remove pions due to their small mass.

